  "solution": {
    "approach": "The binary representation of a non‑negative integer can be obtained by repeatedly dividing the number by 2 and collecting the remainders. The remainders read in reverse order form the binary string. For N=0 the answer is \"0\". This process takes O(log₂N) steps because the number of divisions equals the number of bits. Since each test case is independent we apply the same procedure for all T cases.",
    "timeComplexity": "O(T·log N) – each test case needs at most log₂N divisions (or a single library call).",
    "spaceComplexity": "O(1) extra space besides the output string.",
    "code": {
      "C": "",
      "C++": "",
      "Java": "import java.io.*;\nimport java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));\n        String line = br.readLine();\n        if (line == null || line.isEmpty()) return;\n        int T = Integer.parseInt(line.trim());\n        StringBuilder out = new StringBuilder();\n        for (int i = 0; i < T; i++) {\n            String s;\n            while ((s = br.readLine()) != null && s.isEmpty()) {}\n            if (s == null) break;\n            int N = Integer.parseInt(s.trim());\n            out.append(Integer.toBinaryString(N));\n            if (i + 1 < T) out.append('\\n');\n        }\n        System.out.print(out.toString());\n    }\n}",
      "Python3": "",
      "JavaScript": ""
    },
